{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/wraith-the-oblivion/741267-a-guidebook-to-stygia-chapter-one-the-docks","id":741267,"title":"A Guidebook to Stygia: Chapter one, the Docks","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Wraith: The Oblivion"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-10-30T03:56:17-07:00","id":741267,"title":"A Guidebook to Stygia: Chapter one, the Docks","content":"Forwards<br /> <br /> Welcome to the Isle of Sorrows. If you are reading this book, you are either dead, or are some variety of creature or entity capable of reaching into the Shadowlands. If you are dead, please by all means continue reading. if you are among the Quick, then please return this book to the realm of the Dead posthaste, as it will do you no good, as the living have no need for this tome. <br /> <br /> When Lord Charon vanished in 1945, the city of Stygia was in the middle of a massive crisis, the Shoah wraiths, the 5th Great Malestrom, all manners of conflicts and problems among the Quick came after the war (The Holocaust, Stalinist Purges, the Rwandan Genocide, the War on Terror, AIDS, the list goes on and on and on.) and with the world population increasing in spite of all these changes, it was determined that some method of educating these Wraiths on the finer points of 'life' in the Shadowlands was required. As such, by unanimous decree of the Deathlords, a book was commissioned. <br /> <br /> This book you hold in your hands details the City of Stygia, and while it is by no mean all-encompassing, for the city of the dead has existed since the dawn of human civilization, it does an adequate job of ensuring those who make their way to the Isle of Sorrows have at least some idea of what they are in for and how the city functions. Now, has you have no doubt gathered, you are dead. If you didn't know this, either your Shadow is particularly weak or stupid and hasn't decided to mock you about the fact, or you are in deep seated denial about your situation. <br /> <br /> But the long and short of it is, you died. <br /> <br /> But you yet endure, and you walk despite having passed into death, you think, you feel, you see, you taste, you smell. And in Stygia you can permit your senses to feast on all manner of bygone glories and relics of ages past, you can see sights that mortal historians would kill to behold, hear the ending of the Unfinished Symphony, experience the lost plays of Shakespere (No, he's not down here, but his plays certainly are. We've got a theater made entirely of stacked copies of Romeo and Juliet, I never thought I'd be thanking maudlin teenagers for much of anything, but there you go.) and perhaps, if Fate is on your side, meet long lost loved ones.<br /> <br /> Now there are downsides, the Shadow, the Tempest, hordes of Spectres, Plasmics the size of giants. But I think in time you will find some means of coping with the toils of the Shadowlands, after all if you're reading this book, you where found acceptable by the Hierarchy and permitted to enter the city without your hands clasped in irons as you're dragged to the Forges. Now, as the average Wraith reading this book is no doubt new to the city, if not new to death itself, we will begin with the Docks, the first thing many Wraiths see upon crossing the Tempest, and often times the last. <br /> <br /> - Lady Arakiel, of the Legion of Fate, Anno Stygiae 70 Post Quintum.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Ah the Refugees! Such a heroic spirit, risking all, wagering life itself on a bold, courageous attempt to cross into the unknown in hopes of a better life, to see far shores and wonders, with the greatest wonder of all being a fresh start and glorious new beginnings! Such a shame so many of them die, but they fatten the legions and our coffers of Soulsteel just the same. if Lord Charon where here, we never would of had such a mess of things, he'd of told me to deal with the bastards causing this debacle ages ago, I've wanted an excuse to try out Ebola on a larger scale, but alas, he specifically told me before his battle with Gorol I was to maintain the status quo as best I could, and so I shall.- An excerpt from the Journals of Bone, the Skeletal Lord<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The Docks is where it all begins, for those Wraiths who manage to find their way to Stygia in the tempest (Not an easy feat by any stretch of the imagination, and I applaud you for making it this far) the first thing they see of Stygia is the Buoys on the Sea of Shadows, usually with Moliated criminals bound to them to provide information on the weather and tides. And before long, in the mists of the Tempest, you'll see the Onyx Tower rising from the Isle of Sorrows. <br /> <br /> I myself remember coming here for the first time, it was such a glorious sight not even my Shadow could spoil it. But for others, it's not such a welcome sight, a great many Wraiths come here as Thralls, destined for the Forges. They lack the strength to fight off their Shadows, and to forestall their fall to Oblivion, they are forged into Soulsteel, the backbone of the Stygian economy and the building blocks of Charon's Empire of the Western Dead. Constantly coming and going from the Docks are dozens of ships from all parts of history, Roman Ships pull into dock alongside Ironclads which sank during the 1800s and skirt past World War I era Dreadnoughts making port. Truly, Stygia is a Historians city.<br /> <br /> The first thing anybody notices upon arriving on the Docks is the sheer amount of warehouses, thousands and thousands of them containing all manner of relics still needing to be sorted, all branded with a different seal of the Legions. It's said that the means of stopping the forces of Oblivion and the teeming hordes of the Labyrinth rest in these warehouses, to be found exactly five minutes after its entirely too late to use it. There is nearly a five decade backlog on processing relics, though more obviously important finds are generally scooped up directly by a Deathlord who anticipates the arrival of a choice piece of equipment (The derringer that killed Lincoln, for instance, is currently on display in the Great Museum of Stygia, donated by the Smiling Lord) <br /> <br /> However, Relics are still processed almost constantly, with dozens a day being brought into the city proper after being vetted, cataloged, and the ownership of them being determined. Those that are immediately useful to Stygia are usually purchased from their finders directly, some Wraiths manage to turn a profit skimming the Sea of Shadows for anything from wedding rings to sunken vehicles from some manner of coastline road accident. The relics are fished up or shipped in from further shores, and then brought to shore to be evaluated, with the skimmers paid for their time and effort, with the occasional bonus for rare finds. But with the processing backlog, it can take a great while indeed to be paid, unless the find is immediately apparent as useful (Or a small bribe is paid to a longshoreman) <br /> <br /> From any number of Company Houses, Taverns, Brothels, or even plasm-paper shacks, you can commission a vessel to nearly any part of the Tempest and beyond. As in the days of old, occasionally vessels from the Orient request permission to dock, carrying rare items from the Yellow Springs of the Chinese Dead. Though many of their artifacts are decidedly unsavory (A blanket ban, for instance, exists on the 'eunuchs' of the Yellow Springs within Stygia), some Stygian Nobles pay a great sum for Moliated silk. However, safety while traveling is FAR from certain, even if you happen to be skilled in Argos. The Tempest is a dangerous mistress, as any number of Sailors throughout Stygia will tell you, and many immediately quit the sea upon arriving here, having died an untimely death in its embrace and not desiring a repeat of their death resulting in a painful Harrowing. <br /> <br /> Most recently, there have been a massive upswing in arrivals. You yourself might of been one of these Refugees, crossing from Syria to try to find a new life in Europe, only to sink beneath the waves of the Mediterranean. As you may of gathered from the situation with the Relics, the Admissions Department has been having a hell of a time keeping up on new arrivals. Scores of Wraiths await processing as emissaries from all the Legions argue over which share of Wraiths belong to them. The only Wraiths that AREN'T disputed in this way are those clearly marked by Fate, with the Ladies accepting no other Legion's attempt to 'poach' their dead. The most successful Legion thus far has been the Emerald Legion, with the Emerald Lord's agents arguing that its impossible to tell WHICH ships would sink upon boarding, meaning those who died as a result 'rolled the dice' so to speak, and simply came up with snake eyes. <br /> <br /> A number of Ferrymen, the enigmatic wanderers and alleged psychopomps of the Tempest, have recently arrived as well, protesting Stygia's treatment of these Refugee Wraiths, claiming that this 'Heralds a repeat of the tragedy of the Dark Kingdom of Wire'. But rest assured, the Hierarchy has dealt with far larger gluts when it comes to processing souls, and the Refugee dead are far less numerous then the amount of Wraiths who arrived in the Shadowlands following World War II.<br /> <br /> Finally, we come to the Walls of Stygia, surrounding the city proper and making the 'back wall' of the Docks district. Hierarchy soldiers, clad in armor and carrying weapons of Stygian Steel guard the city from all invaders, intruders, and threats from the Labyrinth. Furthermore, they enforce the laws of Stygia with diligence and a sense of duty instilled by lifetimes of service to their city, should you witness a breach of the law, no matter where you are, find a city guard, and inform them of it. Though I would remind fresh Wraiths not to pester them, as they have their own duties which do not include calling you &quot;Criminal Scum&quot;. <br /> <br /> <br /> Chapter Notes<br /> <br /> You might find yourself wondering &quot;Gee Autochthon, sounds like Stygia is kinda treating the Syrian Refugees like chattel!&quot; Well yes, they are. Stygia is something of a bureaucratic nightmare at the best of times, with the nod of an official determining if its off to the forges or if you get to continue existing...and with the angst this tends to build up, they tend to not be the most kindhearted individuals. Throw in the constant power plays of the Deathlords, and before long, you've got a REAL problem with remembering these Refugees are PEOPLE. <br /> <br /> Of course, this won't be doing any wonder for the Angst levels of dead refugees...<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1645-autochthon","name":"Autochthon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"70"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-10-30T06:02:39-07:00","id":741470,"title":"","content":"Heartily suscribed!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2225-ganonso","name":"ganonso","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2225_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-03T03:35:08-08:00","id":751039,"title":"","content":"10/10, great read.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4572-vo